$\mathbf{\beta} $ -Hairpin and $\mathbf{\beta} $ -sheet structure and stability In recent years, both the turn and strand sequences have been shown to contribute to hairpin structure and stability. The turn sequence is well established to be important for $\upbeta$ -hairpin nucleation, but the $\upbeta$ -strands are also crucial to the formation of a stable $\upbeta$ -hairpin. Two elements contribute to the role of the strands: the intrinsic $\upbeta$ -sheet propensities of the amino acids in the strands and the sidechain–sidechain interactions across the strands. The relative contributions of these components to $\upbeta$ -hairpin stability are still being determined. Through a combination of loop and strand mutations, as well as residue swapping within a strand, Santiveri et al. [1] compared the contributions of turn and strand propensities, and cross-strand sidechain–sidechain interactions. They found that sidechain–sidechain interactions on each face of the hairpin may dominate hairpin stability. This is significant because statistical analyses of protein structures do not account for these effects. Perez-Paya and co-workers [2] investigated the preference for an aromatic versus aliphatic residue at the $_\mathrm{B}{+}1$ position in a type $1^{\prime}$ turn with the sequence Tyr-Asn-Gly-X. Despite the fact that tyrosine is cross-strand from residue $\mathrm{\nabla{X}},$ , the authors found that isoleucine and valine give the most stable $\upbeta$ -hairpin. This result does not correlate with $\upbeta$ -sheet propensities, which the authors suggest is due to the context dependence. In another study, Balaram and co-workers [3] investigated aromatic sidechain–sidechain interactions in organic solvent to explore their significance in the absence of a hydrophobic driving force. They found that aromatic interactions contribute to hairpin stability and concluded that weak electrostatic effects contribute to the interaction in the absence of the hydrophobic effect. This differs from the finding of Nowick and co-workers [4], who used a peptidomimetic $\upbeta$ -sheet system to study aromatic interactions (vide infra). Peptidomimetic systems have also been used to characterize contributions to $\upbeta$ -sheet stability, as described in the next section.
## Introduction
Although the $\upbeta$ -sheet is one of the two most common protein secondary structures, until recently the factors that contributed to $\upbeta$ -sheet structure and stability were poorly understood at best. The discovery of the first modestly folded, monomeric, water-soluble $\upbeta$ -hairpin in 1993 paved the way for the exploration of $\upbeta$ -sheet structure using model systems. The same process had occurred for $\ensuremath{\alpha}$ -helices over a decade earlier, resulting in a set of well-defined rules for the formation of $\propto$ -helices (and subsequently helical bundles) that are still being determined for $\upbeta$ -sheets. With the recent development of several well-folded $\upbeta$ -hairpins, the pace of research has increased significantly during the past year, from fundamental studies of structure and folding to applications such as tissue engineering. This review will cover the latest discoveries from January 2005 to March 2006 on the structure, folding, stability and applications of $\upbeta$ -hairpin and $\upbeta$ -sheet model systems.
Hughes and Waters $[5^{\bullet}]$ recently utilized a $\upbeta$ -hairpin system to investigate the role of lysine methylation in mediating protein–protein interactions (Figure 1a). Methylation of lysine in histone tails triggers the binding of chromodomain proteins, through packing of the methylated lysine within an aromatic pocket, thus modulating chromatin condensation. The interaction of a trimethylated lysine residue with a tryptophan sidechain was investigated within a $\upbeta$ -hairpin to determine the nature of this type of cation– $\pi$ interaction. Within the context of a $\upbeta$ -hairpin peptide, trimethylation of lysine enhances its interaction with tryptophan by about $0.7\mathrm{kcal/mol}$ and imparts significant thermal stability to the $\upbeta$ -hairpin. Thermodynamic analysis of unfolding indicates an enhanced entropic driving force for the Lys–Trp interaction upon methylation. This interaction is a promising source of structural stabilization for hairpin loops within designed proteins.
Chakraborty et al. [6] recently reported the investigation of a four-stranded $\upbeta$ -sheet that was excised from the $\mathrm{HIV}\ \mathrm{gp}120$ protein. This $\upbeta$ -sheet is the binding site for a CD4a-binding antibody, 17b. The binding face of the $\upbeta$ -sheet was maintained while the opposite face of the $\upbeta$ -sheet was varied. $\upbeta$ -Branched hydrophilic residues were incorporated to enhance sheet stability and water solubility. The $\upbeta$ -sheet was found to be approximately $20\%$ folded in water. Incorporation of a disulfide bond had no significant effect. The authors proposed that the lack of a hydrophobic core was the reason for the poor stability.
Although many groups have investigated two-residue turn sequences, until recently there were few reports of a designed three-residue turn in a $\upbeta$ -hairpin. One previously reported example was a G1 bulge in a ubiquitin-based hairpin sequence that led to a non-native strand registry [7]. The Balaram group $[8^{\bullet\bullet}]$ has successfully designed a three-residue turn that does not disrupt native strand registry in methanol (Figure 1b). Starting with a two-residue type $\Pi^{\prime}$ $\upbeta$ -turn, D-Pro–L-Pro, a D-Ala was inserted after the L-Pro, as it favors a left-handed helical conformation. This three-residue turn maintained the $\upbeta$ -hairpin structure, whereas insertion of L-Ala resulted in a two-residue turn with a shift in strand registry. The authors point out that, in aqueous solvent, favorable sidechain–sidechain interactions may play a larger role in determining strand registry.
Several groups have reported new or enhanced methods for characterizing $\upbeta$ -hairpins and $\upbeta$ -sheets. Tokmakoff and colleagues [9] utilized dispersed vibrational echo (DVE) spectroscopy to study $\upbeta$ -hairpins. They found that two amide I vibrational modes persist up to $82^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , suggesting a collapsed structure even at high temperature. Hammer, Keiderling and co-workers [10] investigated the thermal denaturation of $\upbeta$ -hairpins using IR of $^{13}\mathrm{C}.$ - labeled peptides and found that this was a very sensitive method for studying unfolding. Fesinmeyer et al. [11] present a comparative analysis of a number of NMR chemical shift methods, focusing on the quantitative use of amide NH shifts to characterize $\upbeta$ -hairpin structure. Balaram and co-workers [12] reported the use of vibrational circular dichroism (VCD) as a method for characterizing $\propto$ - and $\upbeta$ -secondary structure in the presence of aromatic residues that convolute the interpretation of electronic circular dichroism (ECD). The study indicated that VCD can clearly distinguish between $\scriptstyle\alpha-$ and $\upbeta$ -structure when ECD is ambiguous, although none of the studies reported were performed in aqueous solution.
## $\mathbf{\beta} $ -Hairpin and $\mathbf{\beta} $ -sheet mimics
A number of recent studies have reported the incorporation of peptidomimetics into $\upbeta$ -sheet or $\upbeta$ -hairpin structures. These artificial systems probe all aspects of $\upbeta$ -hairpin structure, including the turn, strands, backbone hydrogen bonding, sidechain–sidechain interactions and chirality. In total, these studies reveal that $\upbeta$ -hairpins are remarkably flexible systems that can tolerate a wide range of alterations.
Bartlett and colleagues $[13,14,15^{\bullet\bullet}]$ have developed an amide isostere consisting of a 1,6-dehydro-3(2H) pyridinone ring for templation of $\upbeta$ -strands (Figure 2a). The so-called $@$ -tide provides rigid templation for $\upbeta$ -sheet formation. The vinylogous amide produces a distinctive CD signal at $280\mathrm{nm}$ that is sensitive to conformational changes, providing a convenient spectroscopic handle for investigating sequence and sidechain effects on $\upbeta$ -hairpin folding. This system has been used to perform an extensive examination of $\upbeta$ -sheet propensities and sidechain– sidechain interaction energies $[15^{\bullet\bullet}]$ . The $@.$ -tide is advantageous relative to proteins because of the lack of complicating tertiary interactions and the unique CD signal, which enables rapid screening of structure. Using the sequence $\mathrm{X}_{1}@\mathrm{VpAVTX}_{2}$ , 77 peptides were investigated, with 11 different residues at the N-terminal position $(\mathrm{X}_{1})$ , including glycine, alanine, valine, isoleucine, leucine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, lysine, asparagine, serine and threonine, and 7 different residues at the C-terminal position $(\mathrm{X}_{2})$ , including glycine, valine, isoleucine, aspartic acid, glutamic acid, lysine and threonine. Aromatic residues were excluded due to potential interference with the CD signal at $280\mathrm{nm}$ . The authors determined the intrinsic stability of each residue compared to that of the glycine substitution. By subtracting the intrinsic stabilities from the overall stabilities, Bartlett and co-workers were able to isolate the contribution of the sidechain–sidechain interaction as well. This excellent system for rapidly assessing the contributions to structure correlates well with a similar study by Smith and Regan [16] done in the context of a protein. An important aspect of their findings is that intrinsic sheet propensities and sidechain–sidechain interactions contribute about equally to overall hairpin stability. Thus, neither can be ignored in $\upbeta$ -hairpin design. For example, for X1–X2 cross-strand pairs such as Glu–Lys, the sidechain–sidechain interaction is favorable, but the intrinsic $\upbeta$ -sheet propensities are low. Hence, the hairpin as a whole is not more stable than the parent sequence containing a Gly–Gly X1–X2 cross-strand pair.
Nowick and co-workers [4] have reported the design of an unnatural amino acid that templates intermolecular $\upbeta$ -sheet formation in organic solvent. This allowed them to investigate the effect of sidechain–sidechain interactions rapidly by measuring the equilibrium between homodimeric and heterodimeric sheets [4]. Within this system, the preference for aromatic–aromatic versus aliphatic–aliphatic cross-strand interactions was investigated, as has been reported previously in the context of a $\upbeta$ -hairpin [17]. Interestingly, no preference for aromatic interactions was observed in this system, although a preference was observed in the $\upbeta$ -hairpin system. Differences in the curvature of the $\upbeta$ -sheet and $\upbeta$ -hairpin were suggested to account for this. Differences in solvent may also influence the disparity of the two systems, although a recent study of aromatic cross-strand interactions in $\upbeta$ -hairpins in organic solvents indicates that aromatic interactions are not screened even when there is no hydrophobic driving force [3]. Using a turn mimic, Fotins and Smithrud [18] also showed that aromatic sidechain– sidechain interactions can dominate hydrogen bonds even in chloroform. In total, these studies indicate that the nature of the peptidomimetic system can influence the outcome of the study.
In another example of a templated $\upbeta$ -sheet, a guanidinopyrrole moiety was incorporated at the $\mathrm{\DeltaN}$ terminus of a tripeptide. This peptidomimetic system was found to bind a tetrapeptide in water via electrostatic and hydrogen-bonding interactions between the guanidinopyrrole and the terminal carboxylate (Figure 2b) [19]. This system demonstrated sequence selectivity based on charge complementarity along the strands.
Kelly and co-workers [20] have investigated the replacement of strand residues in $\mathrm{A}\upbeta$ peptide with alkene isosteres, whereby $\operatorname{C}\upalpha$ and the NH are replaced by an alkene functionality to investigate the role of backbone hydrogen bonding in protein aggregation (Figure 2c). These isosteres have an advantage over an ester because an ester replacement introduces an unfavorable electrostatic interaction between the lone pair on the ester oxygen and the carbonyl in the partner strand. Replacement of the Phe19 and Phe20 residues in $\mathrm{A}\upbeta$ peptide with an alkene isostere resulted in inhibition of amyloid formation, although spherical aggregates still formed, indicating that the Phe19–Phe20 amide bond is critical for fibril formation.
Durani and colleagues $[21^{\bullet\bullet}]$ have investigated the stereochemical tolerance of $\upbeta$ -hairpins by incorporating cross-strand pairs of D-amino acids into the $\upbeta$ -sheet strands. Using a twenty-residue peptide with a D-Pro– Gly turn, two pairs of D-amino acids were incorporated at non-adjacent hydrogen-bonded sites. Remarkably, this design resulted in a structured boat-shaped $\upbeta$ -hairpin with a hydrophobic cluster on the concave face that was folded in aqueous solution. The authors suggest that this design may provide a binding cleft for molecular recognition applications.
Balaram and co-workers [22] have incorporated $\upbeta$ -amino acids into the strands of $\upbeta$ -hairpins and investigated the effect on structural stability. Replacing cross-strand pairs of residues in either hydrogen-bonded or non-hydrogenbonded sites in eight-residue peptides results in folded $\upbeta$ - hairpins in methanol, as determined by NMR and $\mathrm{\DeltaX}$ -ray crystallography. Because these peptides contained only hydrophobic residues, no studies were performed in water.
Two groups have recently reported the incorporation of a photoswitchable azobenzene as the turn sequence (Figure 2d) [23–25]. Both groups found that the cisazobenzene maintained a $\upbeta$ -hairpin structure, but that the trans isomer disrupted folding. The system designed by Hilvert and co-workers was found to aggregate in the trans configuration, whereas no aggregation was observed for either isomer in the system designed by Renner and co-workers. This was attributed to differences in the strand sequences.
The Waters group [26] investigated the effect of synthetic modification of the turn sequence by alkylating the asparagine and lysine residues in a $\upbeta$ -hairpin containing a type $\mathrm{I^{\prime}}$ Val-Asn-Gly-Lys turn sequence. Surprisingly, these changes had only a small effect on overall hairpin stability, suggesting that turn sequences may be more malleable than previously thought. In another example of a modified turn, Balaram and colleagues [27] demonstrated that cyclic $^{\alpha,\alpha}$ -disubstituted amino acids can be placed at the $\mathrm{i}{+}2$ position of a $\upbeta$ -hairpin without disrupting the fold. This is similar to the ability of $\mathbf{\alpha}\propto$ -amino butyric acid (Aib) to stabilize a $\upbeta$ -hairpin, as these disubstituted residues can take on a left-handed helical conformation.
## Experimental kinetics and thermodynamics of $\mathbf{\beta} $ -hairpin folding
Designing proteins and peptides to fold at or near the predicted speed limit for protein folding $(\sim1~\upmu\mathrm{{s})}$ is a challenge for experimentalists. The Kelly group [28] has sought to increase the folding rate of the human Pin1 WW domain, a stable $\upbeta$ -sheet protein that folds in $100~\upmu\mathrm{{s}}$ . Mutations in the turn region of the protein enabled folding rates to be enhanced up to tenfold $(<10~\upmu\mathrm{s})$ . Mutating the wild-type loop region $[29^{\bullet}]$ to type $\mathrm{I^{\prime}}$ and $\mathrm{II^{\prime}}$ turns using asparagine and D-Pro, constraining the loop with flanking cysteine residues and truncating the loop region all resulted in proteins that fold faster than the wild type. The Kelly group expects that these model systems will be useful for computational investigations of folding kinetics.
The effects of turn mutations on the kinetics of $\upbeta$ -sheet folding were also studied in the context of the threestranded $\upbeta$ -sheet peptide VFIVDGOTYTEVDPGOKILQ (where O is ornithine), an analogue of a Gellman sequence, using a novel peptide ‘caging’ strategy. This employs a photolabile linker that traps the peptide in a misfolded conformation $[30^{\bullet}]$ . When the photolabile linker is cleaved, peptide folding is re-initiated from this known initial state. In this study, the folding kinetics of the peptide, which contains a VDGO turn sequence, were compared to those of a stronger turn-nucleating sequence,
TSDPGK. Although the peptides had similar conformational stabilities, folding of the VDGO-containing $\upbeta$ -sheet peptide was slower $(600\mathrm{ns})$ than that of the TSDPGK peptide $(40~\mathrm{ns})$ , leading to the general conclusion that the stronger the turn sequence, the faster the hairpin folds. These results corroborate earlier studies showing that stronger turn sequences enhance the folding rate, whereas stronger hydrophobic clusters decrease the unfolding rate.
In a similar study, the folding kinetics of a trpzip peptide, which contains four tryptophan residues on one face of the hairpin [31] (Figure 1c), were investigated using a laser-induced temperature-jump IR method [32]. Mutations of the hydrophobic core led to increased unfolding rates, whereas mutations of the turn or loop regions primarily affected the folding rate. Additionally, Andersen and co-workers [33] investigated the effects of mutations of the GB1 peptide (GB1P) on the rate of folding using dynamic NMR methodology. Analogous to the aforementioned studies, turn mutations of GB1P (Figure 1d) influenced the rate of hairpin folding, whereas mutations of the hydrophobic core influenced the rate of unfolding.
The Andersen group $[34^{\bullet\bullet}]$ also examined the folding kinetics of the cold-denatured state of a $\upbeta$ -hairpin peptide (MrH3a) and found that they are significantly different from those of the heat-denatured state. Fourier transform (FT) IR difference spectra of the cold- and heat-denatured states indicate that there is significant $\upbeta$ -hairpin structure in the cold-denatured state. This results in an approximately threefold acceleration of the folding rate when starting from the cold-denatured state of the hairpin and has implications for the analysis of temperature-jump studies of protein folding.
In another interesting study, the sensitivities of a $\upbeta$ -hairpin (trpzip, Figure 1c) and an $\upalpha$ -helix (alahel) to the denaturants GdmHCl and urea were compared $[35^{\bullet\bullet}]$ . For proteins, GdmHCl is typically 2–2.5 times more effective than urea as a denaturant. For the $\upbeta$ -hairpin, the ratio was closer to 4:1 for GdmHCl versus urea and close to 1:1 for the $\ensuremath{\alpha}$ -helix. This indicates that different contributions to stability (aromatic clustering in trpzip versus backbone hydrogen bonding in alahel) are not disrupted with equal efficiency by the denaturing agents. It was proposed that GdmHCl is better able to disrupt the hydrophobic core cluster of the peptide hairpin, probably through stacking interactions with the tryptophan residues.
Investigation of the trpzip4 peptide provided the first reported differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) evidence of the two-state folding of a $\upbeta$ -hairpin peptide $[36^{\bullet}]$ . Typically, $\upbeta$ -hairpin thermal transitions are either too broad or $\Delta\mathrm{H}$ is too small to be measured with DSC.
The DSC technique found a $\mathrm{\DeltaT_{m}}$ of $75^{\circ}$ , $\Delta\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{fit}}$ of $94~\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ and $\Delta\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{cal}}$ of $89\mathrm{kJ/mol}$ , which were in good agreement with values of $\mathrm{\DeltaT_{m}}$ $(74^{\circ})$ and $\Delta\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{fit}}$ $(91\mathrm{kJ/mol})$ from CD thermal denaturations. The similarity of $\Delta\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{fit}}$ and $\Delta\mathrm{H}_{\mathrm{cal}}$ from the DSC is proposed to indicate the two-state nature of the folding.
## Computational kinetics and thermodynamics of $\mathbf{\beta} $ -hairpin folding GB1 peptide
Recent developments in the computational simulation of $\upbeta$ -hairpins include the application of replica-exchange molecular dynamics (REMD) simulations to the study of GB1P, the 16-residue $\upbeta$ -hairpin derived from the C-terminal domain of the protein GB1 (Figure 1d) [37]. This particular sequence has garnered a great deal of interest from computational chemists because of its well-characterized kinetic and thermodynamic properties. Experimentally, GB1P has a folding time of $6~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ , well beyond the traditional MD limit of $1~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ $[38^{\bullet}]$ . However, using REMD and Monte Carlo (MC)-based techniques, a number of recent computational studies have achieved results that are in good agreement with experimentally determined kinetic and thermodynamic parameters. One recent study used an MC-based transition path sampling algorithm to reproduce the experimentally determined folding pathway of GB1P (turn formation $\rightarrow$ hydrophobic collapse $\rightarrow$ hydrogen-bond formation). The authors also proposed a role for solvent as a ‘lubricant’ in the folding process, based on the layering of water molecules around the hairpin sidechains in their simulations. Calculations of folding and unfolding rates for the hairpin produced results that are in good agreement with experimental values, although their calculated rates have large margins of error.
GB1P is thought to possess a minor $\upalpha$ -helical conformer in addition to its major $\upbeta$ -hairpin conformation. Application of the T-WHAM (temperature weighted histogram analysis method) model to GB1P by Levy and co-workers $[39^{\bullet\bullet}]$ enabled estimation of the relative free energies of the major $\upbeta$ -hairpin state and the minor $\mathbf{\alpha}\propto$ -helical state at room temperature. According to defined structural criteria, the $\upbeta$ -hairpin population is $88\%$ versus an $\ensuremath{\alpha}$ -helical population of $8\%$ , and the calculated free energy difference between the $\upbeta$ -hairpin and $\upalpha$ -helical states is $1.573\pm0.018\mathrm{kcal/mol}$ . Levy and co-workers $[40^{\bullet}]$ also applied a kinetic network model to an REMD simulation of GB1P. Simulation of temperature-jump experiments reproduced the experimentally observed two-state folding of the peptide and enabled observation of $\upalpha$ -helical intermediates along the folding pathway. This is in contrast to other studies of GB1P, in which $\upalpha$ -helical intermediates have not been observed [41].
Another simulation of GB1P used an essential dynamics analysis of the free energy surface produced by a long traditional MD trajectory $(1.1~\upmu\mathrm{s})$ in explicit aqueous solvent with the software package GROMACS [42]. This approach has the advantage of being able to extrapolate long-interval kinetic behavior (several microseconds) from shorter simulations (less than $1~\upmu\mathrm{s}\dot$ . This simulation was able to accurately reproduce the experimentally measured folding kinetics of the hairpin $[5~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ (simulated) versus $6~\upmu\mathrm{s}$ (experimental) relaxation time].
## Other peptide simulations
A number of other $\upbeta$ -sheet-forming peptides have undergone computational analysis. For example, the folding of the amyloidogenic AH1 peptide (MKHMAGAAAAGAVV) from the Syrian hamster prion protein was studied in explicit aqueous solution at $300\mathrm{K}$ using a long MD simulation $(1.1~\upmu\mathrm{s})$ with the GROMACS software package and the GROMOS96 force-field [43]. Starting from an $\upalpha$ -helical conformation, the sequence folded into a $\upbeta$ -hairpin with a very short average folding time $(200~\mathrm{ns})$ ; this may be indicative of its tendency to aggregate with other peptides into more stable fibrillar structures rather than form the less stable $\upbeta$ -hairpin monomer.
A three-stranded $\upbeta$ -sheet peptide (DPDP) from the Gellman laboratory was characterized by both traditional MD and REMD simulations $[44^{\bullet\bullet}]$ . The goal of this study was to model the cooperative folding of the two hairpins that compose the three-stranded $\upbeta$ -sheet. REMD studies revealed that DPDP occupies at least four states, consistent with experimental observations, and reproduced the relative stabilities of the two hairpins. Although the calculated value of about $-3.3\left(\pm0.5\right)\mathrm{kcal/mol}$ for hairpin cooperativity is larger than that observed experimentally, the authors recognize that the experimental values underestimate the effect of cooperativity.
A recent study used a 250 ns simulation of a $\upbeta$ -hairpin with GROMACS to examine the role of hydrophobic interactions and salt bridges in $\upbeta$ -hairpin folding [45]. The authors show the importance of hydrophobic interactions to the initiation of peptide folding using the 13- residue peptide WRYYESSLEPYPD. Mutation of just one of the residues in the hydrophobic core (formed by aromatic residues W1, Y3, Y4 and Y11) to alanine produced a mutant peptide, WRYYESSLEPAPD, that would not fold in silico.
The effect of various force-field parameters on MD simulations remains an active area of interest, as different treatments of electrostatics and polarizability in molecular simulations can result in very different outcomes. For example, the effects of incorporating polarizability into the force-field of a free energy simulation were tested on simulations of the trpzip2 peptide $[46^{\bullet\bullet}]$ . This study shows that aromatic multipoles must be included in order to replicate the orientation of the tryptophan rings found in the NMR solution structures. Similarly, a recent study of a force-field with simplified interaction potentials gave good results for the folding of GB1P variants, but did not fold trpzip into its experimentally observed conformation, further indicating the importance of sidechain polarization to the structure of the trpzip peptides [47]. Another study measured the influence of different electrostatic treatments (GRE, PME, regular cutoffs) on the conformation of a peptide during a simulation (sequence YQNPDGSQA) [48]. Use of regular cutoffs enabled the simulation to dig deeper into conformational space. Also investigated was the influence of sequence-based versus structure-based energy functions on the folding of a 20-residue peptide [49]. Although both models reproduce two-state behavior, it is shown that each model differs in its reproduction of various thermodynamic quantities, including the free energy landscape. These results corroborate earlier computational studies showing that, although different models may correctly reproduce a native fold, their kinetics and thermodynamics may differ significantly.
## $\mathbf{\beta}_{\mathbf{\beta}}$ -Hairpins in protein structure
In a related study, the effect of swapping turn sequences from different regions of human acidic fibroblast growth factor (FGF-1) was investigated. In the wild-type protein, the $\upbeta4/\upbeta5$ turn has a type II/IV conformation, whereas the $\mathsf{\beta8}/\mathsf{\beta9}$ turn has a type I G1 $\upbeta$ -bulge. Surprisingly, swapping the sequences did not result in conformational swapping of the turns, indicating that the sequence did not define the turn conformation in this system [53]. The turns also retained their respective conformations when substituted with glycine sequences. This study points to the role of the turn-flanking residues in determining turn conformation and supports a hypothesis from the Searle group, which is that turn regions in proteins may be able to maintain their conformation despite variations in their primary sequence [7].
Sugiura and colleagues [54] have investigated the role of the $\upbeta$ -hairpin in zinc finger domains by performing a $\upbeta$ -hairpin swap between two zinc fingers, Sp1(zf23) and GL1(zf45). They found that the Sp1(zf23) mutant bound its target DNA about tenfold better than the natural system, whereas the mutant GL1(zf45) showed no binding. This demonstrates the significant role that the $\upbeta$ -hairpin structure plays in DNA recognition.
Mutation of a $\upbeta$ -hairpin sequence in $\upalpha$ -lytic protease to a more favorable turn sequence (AEGA to GGGDV) was shown enhance its kinetic stability by making unfolding slower [55]. This is in contrast to the behavior observed in monomeric $\upbeta$ -hairpin sequences, whereby favorable turn mutations generally enhance the folding rate. Mutation of the hairpin turn also resulted in decreased proteolytic activity, which suggests the evolutionary importance of the non-rigidity of protein structure for function. In another study, disruption of a salt bridge in the $\upbeta$ -hairpin of the human $\mathrm{\DeltaT}$ cell leukemia virus capsid protein destabilized the protein and affected its structure and activity, but not its ability to recognize antibodies, which occurs in a precursor state in which the hairpin is not formed [56]. Finally, a computational and experimental investigation of a $\upbeta$ -hairpin in $\mathsf{\beta}\mathsf{B}2$ -crystallin showed that a cysteine to phenylalanine mutation within the $\upbeta$ -hairpin enhanced the stability of the protein and slowed its rate of unfolding [57]. This result corroborates studies of monomeric $\upbeta$ -hairpin systems in that an enhanced hydrophobic cluster slows unfolding. It is proposed that the destabilizing cysteine residue may be important to flexibility in the $\upbeta$ -crystalline dimerization interface.
## De novo design of proteins containing $\mathbf{\beta}$ -sheets
Although the number of designed proteins containing $\upbeta$ -sheet structure lags behind those containing $\upalpha$ -helices, several recent examples have been reported. Dutton, DeGrado and co-workers $[58^{\bullet\bullet}]$ have designed a mimic of the redox protein rubredoxin, a $\upbeta$ -sandwich protein (Figure 3). The mimic incorporates the two $\upbeta$ -hairpins that make up the metal-binding site, which are linked by a trpzip $\upbeta$ -hairpin. It also contains an overhand connection
Connectivity of the $\upbeta$ -barrel structure of rubredoxin and the rubredoxin mimic; the red section represents the trpzip $\upbeta$ -hairpin.
that incorporates a third strand in each $\upbeta$ -sheet to enhance the hydrophobic core. Investigation of its redox properties showed that it can undergo redox cycling at least 16 times, compared to 2–3 times for previously reported rubredoxin mimics. The authors attribute this to its enhanced stability. This is one of only a few examples of a functional de novo designed $\upbeta$ -sheet protein.
Keating, Imperiali and co-workers $[59^{\bullet}]$ have used computational approaches to convert a designed homotetramer containing mixed $\scriptstyle\alpha-$ and $\upbeta$ -structure into the first designed $\beta\beta\alpha$ heterotetramer by replacing the leucine residues in the hydrophobic interior with a combination of phenylalanine and alanine residues. The heterotetrameric structure will allow incorporation of different peptide units with different properties in future designs.
In another example of a mixed $\upalpha/\upbeta$ system, Sahal and coworkers [60] reported a conjugated $\upbeta$ -hairpin/helix system in which the trpzip hairpin sequence was linked to a dehydrophenylalanine helical octapeptide via a tetraglycine linker. The helix alone is insoluble in water, but linkage to the $\upbeta$ -hairpin produces a water-soluble peptide with tertiary interactions between the helix and hairpin.
A number of designed systems that can be induced to switch between $\upalpha$ -helical and $\upbeta$ -sheet structures have been reported recently. Koksch and co-workers have reported two systems that switch from an $\upalpha$ -helix to a $\upbeta$ -sheet, triggered by $\mathrm{pH}\ [61^{\bullet}]$ and by metal ions [62]. These systems were designed to investigate the molecular interactions that may influence the misfolding and aggregation of proteins involved in diseases such as Alzheimer’s, Parkinson’s and prion-type diseases. The design involved modifying a standard $\upalpha$ -helical coiled-coil by maintaining the hydrophobic core at the a and d sites of the helix heptad repeat $\left(\mathrm{a-g}\right)$ , while mutating other positions to favor a $\upbeta$ -sheet. In the case of the $\mathrm{\pH}$ switchable system, $\upbeta$ -sheet-stabilizing valine residues were incorporated at the b, c and f sites, which do not contribute significantly to the coiled-coil dimer, but are favorable for $\upbeta$ -sheet formation. In addition, lysine residues were incorporated at the b and f sites, such that, at low $\mathrm{\pH}$ , charge–charge repulsion will destabilize the $\upalpha$ -helix, inducing a switch to the $\upbeta$ -sheet. Indeed, by combining $\mathrm{pH}$ and concentration effects, this system could be switched from random coil to $\upalpha$ -helical coiledcoil to $\upbeta$ -sheet aggregates. The metal-binding system was similar in design, with valine residues at the b, c and f positions and four histidines incorporated into the sequence to form a metal-binding site when the peptide is in a $\upbeta$ -sheet conformation. In $40\%$ TFE, the peptide exhibited helical character, but in the presence of a metal ion, $\upbeta$ -sheet structure was induced. A control peptide containing serine in place of histidine showed no metalion-induced structural change. This study provides support for the possible role of metal ions in protein misfolding.
The Woolfson $[63^{\bullet}]$ and Kuhlman [64] groups have independently reported a metal-induced switch from a three-helix bundle to a $\beta\beta\alpha$ zinc finger domain that consists of a metal-binding site between the $\upbeta$ -hairpin and $\upalpha$ -helix. In each case, the addition of zinc nucleated the formation of the $\beta\beta\alpha$ motif necessary to create a metal-binding site, at the expense of the three-helix bundle. These studies demonstrate that protein structure is not always defined by its primary sequence alone.
## Functional peptides containing $\mathbf{\beta}_{\mathbf{\beta}}$ -hairpins or $\mathbf{\beta} $ -sheets
A number of research groups have begun to create functional peptides based on a $\upbeta$ -sheet or $\upbeta$ -hairpin scaffold. One approach has been to mimic protein domains, as in the work from the Gellman and Waters groups. On the basis of structural and mutational studies of the Yap WW domain, which binds a polyproline helix, Gellman and colleagues [65] designed a $\upbeta$ -hairpin mimic that incorporated favorable structural elements, such as a D-Pro–Gly turn sequence, along with the residues that have been identified as participating in binding. Surprisingly, no binding was observed, despite the fact that all of the purported key residues from the protein were present, demonstrating the subtleties of molecular recognition.
Waters and co-workers have designed a $\upbeta$ -hairpin peptide based on the OB-fold (oligonucleotide/oligosaccharidebinding fold) and RRM (RNA recognition motif) domains, which bind single-stranded DNA and RNA through a combination of aromatic stacking interactions and electrostatic interactions. A $\upbeta$ -hairpin peptide with two tryptophan residues and two lysine residues on its binding face was found to bind nucleotide triphosphates with binding affinities in the mid-micromolar range, but with little selectivity for any one nucleotide [66]. A dimer of this $\upbeta$ -hairpin was then designed to bind short single-stranded oligonucleotides in the low micromolar range, which is similar to the affinity of the simplest of the OB-fold domains $[67^{\bullet}]$ . This is an excellent model system for determining the minimum requirements for biomolecular recognition.
Schneider and co-workers have previously reported a switchable $\upbeta$ -hairpin system that aggregates to form a hydrogel upon folding. The 20-residue peptide consists of alternating hydrophobic (valine) and hydrophilic (lysine) residues, and a turn in the middle of the sequence. Folding can be induced by a $\mathrm{\pH}$ change or by salt screening, which reduces charge–charge repulsion between lysine residues. Recent studies of this system have shown that culture media provide the necessary salt screen to induce folding and hydrogel formation $[68^{\bullet}]$ . This hydrogel was then shown to be compatible with NIH 3T3 murine fibroblast cells, enabling both cell attachment and cell proliferation, without any sort of functionalization. This appears to be a promising system for tissue engineering. In addition, the authors have designed a photocaged system that can be triggered upon exposure to light, providing spatial and temporal control of hydrogelation. Furthermore, the Schneider group [69] has found that they can tune the physical properties of the hydrogel simply by switching the valine residues from the hydrogen-bonding sites to the non-hydrogen-bonding sites of the $\upbeta$ -hairpin. This promises to be an extremely versatile system for biomaterials applications.
## Conclusions
With the development of well-folded $\upbeta$ -hairpin model systems, our fundamental understanding of $\upbeta$ -sheet structure and folding has advanced considerably in the past year, with a wealth of structural, kinetic and thermodynamic studies as well as conformational modeling. The design rules for making stable, well-folded $\upbeta$ -sheet systems are beginning to take shape, such that we will soon be able to design such systems as easily as one can design an $\mathbf{\alpha}\propto$ -helical system today. Research areas are now expanding to the development of functional $\upbeta$ -hairpin and $\upbeta$ -sheet systems, with some early success. The field is now well on its way to mastering the design of structures containing $\upbeta$ -sheets.
## Update
A number of noteworthy articles have been published since this review was submitted. Andersen et al. $[70^{\bullet}]$ have published a detailed paper investigating the effect of turn and strand residues on $\upbeta$ -hairpin stability, including a comparison of thermodynamic data from a number of previously reported systems. Mun˜ oz et al. [71] have published a similarly broad paper on the kinetics of hairpin folding, supporting a turn nucleation mechanism for folding, rather than a hydrophobic collapse mechanism. Two additional computational papers have been published that also explore folding mechanisms of $\upbeta$ -hairpins. In the work by Imamura and Chen [72], MC simulations are used to investigate possible folding pathways for GB1P, including the effect on folding of the proximity of a hydrophobic cluster to the turn sequence. Four folding pathways are found to dominate the simulations: zipping out, middle out, zipping in and reptation, which is the formation of a misregistered fold that eventually rearranges to the native state. In a study of a trpzip peptide using REMD simulations, Pitera et al. [73] found little evidence of reptation in silico at $425\mathrm{~K~}$ . Lastly, Gai and co-workers [74] reported a time-resolved IR study coupled with a laser-induced temperature-jump technique to investigate the differences between the cold- and heat-denatured states of a $\upbeta$ -hairpin. A three-state model for folding/unfolding is proposed to be more appropriate than the traditional two-state model for $\upbeta$ -hairpins exhibiting cold denaturation.
